LIMITATION
Enemy of Eternity: Renichi has an innate curse that causes every living being in any world to instinctively despise and fear him—those who sense him harbor hostility toward him. A powerful curse so intense that children cry, dogs flee, and carriages overturn and monstrous creatures survival instincts are triggered merely from his presence. The curse so strong that those who are Killers or those impulses feel an urge to kill him like he’s their ideal target. Even those who are considered Saints with the biggest heart of forgiveness will feel a crippling unease from being near him unless he reassure them to partially ease the tension. There are some exceptions: Those are considered one of highest existence like Ultimate Ones, Divine Spirits, Archetype: Earth, Root-Connected Individuals, are immune. Some who individuals possess the mental fortitude to overcome the overwhelming curse, not fully but likely up 50-95% resistance to it. Those who are related to him are completely immune.

Psychological Attachment to Ordinary Life: Renichi’s greatest emotional vulnerability is his attachment to ordinary life and the people who make it meaningful. His desire to preserve peaceful moments originates from his fear of losing the everyday existence he cherishes. He does not naturally seek conquest, transcendence, or absolute power, and his reluctance to abandon his humanity can create hesitation when achieving victory requires sacrificing something precious. Enemies who threaten ther people important to him can force him into situations where his emotional priorities conflict with tactical efficiency. This does not mean he will automatically surrender when someone is taken hostage; rather, his attachment creates opportunities for manipulation and forces him to consider consequences beyond simply defeating his opponent. Even after becoming a god, the fundamental contradiction remains: he possesses the authority to impose his desire upon existence, yet what he truly wants is to protect the ordinary moments that divine conflicts continually destroy.

Reluctance to Manifest His Hegemony Law:
Before fully accepting his Hegemony Law, Renichi hesitates to emanate his desire across creation because doing so entails imposing his personal desire upon the entire world. His wish to preserve the present is emotionally understandable, but a universally enforced eternal moment can prevent existence from progressing according to the wishes of other individuals. Renichi’s concern about this consequence contributes to his reluctance to exercise his divine authority. His eventual discussions are significant in allowing him to confront this hesitation. The vulnerability lies in the difference between possessing the capacity to manifest a Law and being psychologically prepared to accept what that manifestation entails. This is primarily an obstacle in his development and should not be described as a permanent inability to activate Law after he resolves the conflict.

Metaphysical Dependence on Mercurius: Renichi’s unusual existence originates from his connection to Mercurius, who is both his creator and the foundation of important aspects of his supernatural nature. This relationship is especially significant for his divine existence. Certain character references identify Mercurius's death as a condition that causes Renichi to lose his divinity, making the continued existence of his creator a profound metaphysical vulnerability in those circumstances. However, this should not be generalized into a claim that every injury inflicted upon Mercurius damages Renichi, that killing Mercurius automatically produces the same result in every route, or that Renichi’s entire existence is nothing more than an expendable extension of his creator. The relevant weakness concerns the specific metaphysical relationship supporting his divinity. It’s only that killing Mercurius, Renichi may lose of half of his Throne Godhood and can only regain all of it if he sits on 「The Throne」.

Soul Consumption and Energy Management: Renichi’s abilities normal operates through the collection and utilization of souls, with the accumulated stock contributing to a supernatural strength and continued activity. Renichi is an exceptional case because his unusual relationship with Mercurius grants him advantages unavailable to ordinary practitioners. His connection to Mercurius’s Tremendous Soul compensates for his comparatively limited conventional soul stock that require him to take from human life. Consequently, describing Renichi as someone who can simply exhaust a small collection of ordinary souls and immediately become powerless would be misleading. His energy requirements and resources change between transformations, particularly when he acquires greater abilities. Scherzo's requirement to draw upon his own soul is a clearer example of an explicitly demonstrated resource complication.

The Eternal Visitor Paradox: It is a profound psychological contradiction within Renichi Eskaphy, born from the conflict between his deepest desire to preserve the moments he cherishes and his deeply ingrained inability to imagine himself permanently belonging within them. Despite his reserved demeanor, extraordinary psychological insight, and apparent emotional detachment, Renichi genuinely treasures the simplicity of ordinary existence, finding happiness in familiar places, peaceful routines, quiet companionship, and the seemingly insignificant moments shared with others. His Origin of Stagnation reflects this longing, culminating in the Craving that forms the foundation of his Hegemony Law, Also Sprach Zarathustra: the desire to remain within a cherished moment forever, preventing its beauty and significance from being lost to the passage of time. Yet Renichi possesses a peculiar psychological habit that fundamentally contradicts this desire. Throughout his life, he has unconsciously divided the people he encounters into those he can comfortably remain beside and those he cannot remain with for long, while rarely considering a third possibility: those he genuinely wishes to stay with. Although he is exceptionally skilled at interpreting human behavior, recognizing concealed emotions, and understanding the motivations of others, his insight does not extend naturally to acknowledging his own place in their lives. He may recognize affection, experience genuine attachment, and become deeply important to another person without ever considering that their relationship could become something permanent. This does not arise from an inability to love, emotional immaturity, or a conscious belief that he is undeserving of happiness. Rather, Renichi has become so accustomed to existing as a temporary presence in the lives of others that departure feels like an inevitable conclusion rather than a decision he is free to reconsider. His personal principle, “Knowing isn’t permission,” further reinforces this tendency, as he refuses to interpret his understanding of another person’s feelings as an entitlement to their companionship, affection, or future. Even when he correctly suspects that someone wishes for him to remain, he does not automatically assume that he has been invited to do so, and his respect for personal autonomy can coexist with an almost complete failure to recognize that he himself is equally entitled to express the desire to stay. Consequently, Renichi can cherish someone without imagining a shared future, experience happiness without attempting to preserve it through ordinary human commitment, and leave behind relationships that might have endured simply because he never consciously considered remaining. This contradiction becomes especially significant when expressed through his divine nature. His Hegemony Law possesses the authority to impose absolute stagnation upon creation, preserving existence within an eternal, unchanging moment, yet the very individual who desires such permanence struggles to envision himself as a lasting participant in the happiness he wishes to preserve. During the earlier stages of his development, this unresolved contradiction interferes with the complete manifestation of his Law, not because his divine authority is inherently insufficient, but because his conscious understanding of himself remains fundamentally misaligned with the Craving from which that authority originates. He desires an eternal moment while instinctively preparing to depart from it, seeking permanence in existence without recognizing the possibility of permanence in his own relationships. Furthermore, his reluctance to impose his desires upon others introduces an ethical dimension to the paradox, since he understands that preserving his happiness through universal stagnation would deprive countless individuals of their freedom to change, grow, and pursue futures of their own choosing. Ultimately, the Eternal Visitor Paradox does not describe a man incapable of forming lasting bonds, but someone who has never fully learned to regard permanence as a possibility he may freely choose. Its resolution lies neither in abandoning his respect for others nor in forcing himself to remain somewhere against his wishes, but in recognizing that companionship can be mutually desired, that expressing a wish to stay does not constitute possession, and that departure need not be the inevitable conclusion of every meaningful encounter. Renichi’s greatest psychological obstacle is therefore not learning how to preserve a moment, for he already possesses the divine authority to accomplish that on a cosmic scale, but learning that he may choose to continue living within it alongside the people he cherishes. The tragedy of the Eternal Visitor is that a being capable of denying the progression of all creation has spent much of his existence overlooking a far simpler possibility: he does not always have to leave.

